.TH GRAPW 1
.SH NAME
grapw, Grap \- draw grap's graphs and edit their data with the mouse
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grapw
[
.B -p
]
.I file
.br
.B Grap
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Grapw
draws a
.IR grap (1)
graph in a
.IR rio (1)
window: its frame, ticks, grid and labels, and a point for each number in its rows of data,
joined as its
.B draw
statements say.
Each point knows the line it came from, so selecting points selects their lines,
and moving a point rewrites its numbers in its line and nothing else.
.PP
The graph is the text between the first
.B .G1
and the
.B .G2
after it, and
.I grapw
keeps the rest of the file as it is, so
.B "grap file | pic | troff"
prints it.
A file with no
.B .G1
is all graph; it prints through
.BR "{echo .G1; cat file; echo .G2} | grap" .
If
.I file
doesn't exist, the graph starts empty, and
.B w
makes it.
.PP
.I Grapw
places things as
.I grap
does, but draws only part of its language:
.BR frame ,
.BR ticks ,
.BR grid ,
.BR label ,
.B coord
with
.B x
and
.B y
ranges,
.B draw
and
.BR new ,
variables set to numbers, such as
.B frameht
and
.BR margin ,
and rows of numbers, separated by spaces or commas, one to a line or several separated by
.BR ; ;
selecting a point selects its row, so rows that share a line are selected apart.
A row of one number is a point whose x counts the rows; a row of several is an x
and the y of a point for each of the rest.
.I Grapw
keeps lines of
.I pic
or
.I troff
but doesn't draw them.
For anything else, such as a loop, a macro, a log scale, a named coordinate system,
or a
.BR line ,
.B circle
or
.BR plot ,
.I grapw
names it and its line on standard error and exits with status
.BR subset .
.PP
With
.BR -p ,
.I grapw
draws nothing: it prints the frame's size, the mapping from data to inches,
each tick and grid line with its label, and each point with its line.
.SS Mouse
.TF button\ 3
.TP
button 1
A click selects the row of the point under the mouse; a sweep selects the rows of the points in the box.
With Shift, a click adds a row or takes it out, and a sweep adds.
A click at nothing clears the selection.
.TP
button 2
Drag a point.
Letting go rewrites the numbers in its line: its y, and its x unless the row is one number;
a new x moves every point of the row.
.I Grapw
keeps a number the mouse moved less than 4 pixels along.
A new number has as many decimals as a pixel is worth, and no more than its column has anywhere
in the graph, so whole numbers stay whole;
a number written with an exponent, such as
.BR 2e-3 ,
keeps one, with as many digits as a pixel is worth.
Escape or another button takes the drag back.
.TP
button 3
The menu:
.BR Cut ,
.BR Copy ,
.BR Paste ,
.BR Undo ,
.BR Redo ,
.B Write
and
.BR Exit .
.PD
.PP
The message line shows the line of the point under the mouse,
or the x and y of the mouse in the frame.
.SS Keys
.TF Command-Shift-Z
.TP
.BR Delete ", Backspace"
Delete the selected rows, keeping what shares their lines.
.TP
.BR Command-Z ", control-Z"
Undo; it keeps the last hundred changes.
.TP
.BR Command-Shift-Z ", Command-Y, control-Y"
Redo.
.TP
.BR Command-C ", control-C"
Copy the selected rows to
.BR /dev/snarf ,
as
.IR Grap 's
pipes send them.
.TP
.BR Command-X ", control-X"
Copy, then delete.
.TP
.BR Command-V ", control-V"
Paste the snarf's lines after the last selected row, or after the last row, and select them.
.TP
.BR Command-A ", control-A"
Select every row.
.TP
Escape
Clear the selection.
.TP
.BR w ", Command-S"
Write the file.
.TP
.B q
Exit; with changes not written, the first says so and the second exits.
.PD
.SS Grap
.I Grap
is
.I grapw
on an
.IR acme (1)
window, whose body is the graph's text.
With no
.IR file ,
.I Grap
takes the window it runs in,
.BR $winid ;
with one, outside a window, or in a window whose
.B event
another program holds through
.IR amux (4),
as
.I win
does, it opens a new window, with the file's text if it exists.
The window stays text, to edit as any other;
.I Grap
draws the graph in a
.IR rio (1)
window of its own, opened by
.I newwindow
(see
.IR graphics (2))
on
.BR $wsys ,
under a tag as Acme's
.RI ( adraw (2))
that holds the file's name,
.BR "Del Snarf |" ,
and
.BR "Undo Redo Put" ,
and its tag's square shows whether the window has changes Put has not written.
Each change writes only the lines it changed into the body, so
.B Put
saves it, and diffs stay small.
Dot is the selected rows' lines when they are one range of whole lines,
and otherwise an empty dot at the first selected row;
the rows on dot's lines start selected,
and when the selection empties, dot becomes the point after what it covered.
When something else changes the body, such as
.B Edit
or another program,
.I Grap
reads it again, keeping selected the rows on lines that didn't change,
and
.B Undo
takes the change back;
while the text is outside the subset,
.I Grap
keeps showing the last drawing it could make, in a border, with the reason and its line,
says the same in
.BR +Errors ,
and takes no edits of the drawing,
until an edit or
.B Undo
makes the text one it can draw again.
If the body changes while
.I Grap
writes a change of its own, as when a pipe's command is still running,
.I Grap
writes nothing, says
.I file
.BR "changed; try again" ,
and reads the body again.
.PP
.I Grap
adds itself to the text window's tag.
.B Grap
there ends it and closes its window;
.B Grap
again draws it, or says in
.B +Errors
why it cannot.
.I Grap
ends too, and its window closes, when the text window is gone.
In
.IR Grap 's
tag,
.BR Undo ,
.BR Redo
and the menu's other commands are
.IR grapw 's,
.BR Put ,
.B Write
and
.B w
write
.B put
to the text window's
.BR ctl ,
.BR Del ,
.B Exit
and
.B q
end it as
.B Grap
does, other commands run as in an Acme tag, with
.B $winid
the text window, and these commands run in the file's directory.
They work on the selection as text:
the selected rows, one to a line in the file's order, a row joined to another by
.B ;
on a line of its own.
When the selection takes in rows of more than one series,
each series' rows follow its
.B draw
or
.B new
line, as the file has it.
.TP
.BI | cmd
Send the selection through
.IR cmd .
Its output is the selection's new version, read the same way.
When a series has as many rows in it as the series had selected,
each takes the place of the selected row it matches in order,
on that row's line, so an unselected row between two selected ones stays between them,
and
.I Grap
selects only those rows.
Otherwise the series' rows replace its selected rows
as one block where the first of them was;
.I Grap
takes out a row the output leaves out, even from a line it shares by
.BR ; .
The rest of the file keeps its text, layout and order.
With nothing selected, as with
.IR acme 's
.BR | ,
.I cmd
gets nothing, and its rows go in at the insertion point:
dot's line if dot is in the graph, else after the last row.
.I Grap
selects what comes back.
.TP
.BI < cmd
Replace the selection with
.IR cmd 's
output in the same way, as
.IR acme 's
.B <
replaces a selection, or with nothing selected put it at the insertion point.
.TP
.BI > cmd
Send the selection to
.IR cmd ,
or nothing if none is selected, as
.IR acme 's
.B >
does; its output goes to
.BR +Errors .
.PD
.PP
As
.IR acme 's
pipes do,
.I Grap
writes what
.I cmd
prints whatever its exit status, and a failing status goes to
.BR +Errors .
Output with a line that isn't a row or one of the selection's series lines
takes the selected rows' place as it is, where the first of them was.
A rewrite is one change to the body, so
.IR acme 's
.B Undo
takes it back.
.IR Acme 's
.B Edit
works on dot as always:
.B "Edit ,>cmd"
sends the whole text, and
.B "Edit ,|cmd"
changes it.
.B Del
and
.B Delete
in the text window's tag go to the window's
.B ctl
as
.B del
and
.BR delete ,
so
.B Del
on a window changed since it was last written says
.IB file " modified"
and keeps the window and the drawing, as
.IR acme 's
.B Del
does, and a second
.BR Del ,
with no change between, or
.BR Delete ,
deletes the window, ending
.I Grap
with it.
.SH EXAMPLES
In
.IR Grap 's
tag, with the outliers selected:
.IP
.EX
|awk '$2 < 100'
.EE
.PP
With every row selected (Command-A), sort them by x; then, with nothing selected, add squares:
.IP
.EX
|sort -n
<seq 10 | awk '{print $1, $1*$1}'
.EE
.PP
Typeset the graph; the second line is for a graph with no
.BR .G1 :
.IP
.EX
Edit ,>grap | pic | troff >/tmp/graph.out
Edit ,>{echo .G1; cat; echo .G2} | grap | pic | troff >/tmp/graph.out
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/grapw
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR grap (1),
.IR pic (1),
.IR sketch (1),
.IR adraw (2),
.IR acme (4)
.SH BUGS
.I Grap
writes a rewrite of more than 4096 bytes in pieces, so an edit by something else between two pieces
leaves part of the rewrite in the body.
.br
.I Acme
guards a window's body for one program at a time:
another program that guards its writes or reads of the same window, as
.IR hl (1)
does, can take
.IR Grap 's
guard, so a change can be refused when nothing else changed the body.
.br
.I Grapw
draws text in the screen's font, not troff's, so it loses sizes and most escapes,
and it draws a mark other than a bullet, box, square, circle, star, plus, times, delta, dot, or tick
as its text.
